// --- The three crosses ---
//
// Each of the west, north and south walls is built in two layers: a back
// plate, and a front layer 0.8 m proud of it laid out as eight pieces around
// a cross shaped void. The upright stands 35.2 m and the bar reaches 25.6 m
// across, both cut 6.4 m thick, so what shows is a thin figure drawn on a
// broad wall rather than a blocky one. The bar sits a little over a quarter
// of the way up the
// upright rather than the same distance down from the top, which turns the
// cross over.
//
// The bar is placed in whole lozenge rows. A row is 0.8 m -- a quarter of the
// 3.2 m a panel fits -- so the bar can only be moved by multiples of 0.8 m
// without its rows falling out of step with the upright's.
//
// The void is filled with lamp panels laid on the back plate, so each cross
// reads as a slot of red light rather than a shadow. Those panels are cut into
// 3.2 m squares, one per texture, and each is marked `fit`: the lamp texture is
// 128 texels across and lays its eight lozenges out on a 64 by 32 texel grid
// inside that, so a panel whose edges miss those lines slices the lozenges at
// its ends. A limb is two panels across at this size, and the void is filled
// two columns wide rather than one. The room is centred on z 29.6 and x -59.2,
// neither of which the grid divides, so tiling by world position cannot be
// made to land -- `fit` maps one whole copy across each square instead and
// puts the cut where the panels already end.
//
// The upright and the arms are cut as separate squares that meet rather than
// overlap, so no two lamp faces are coplanar.

// West wall
